Authorization headers are not allowed by cross-origin resource sharing (CORS). Any header set on the preflight request is immediately stripped by the server before the request is passed to the origins server.

You cannot include an authorization header in the initial request, as it will be blocked by the browser

However, the Access-Control-Allow-Credentials header, when set to true, allows user credentials (including cookies and HTTP authentication headers) to be sent in cross-origin requests. Including this header tells the browser to allow credentials for the specified origin. This can be accomplished by making a preflight request with an HTTP GET Method to the same URL as your actual request, which should return a status code of 200. After this, your actual request will include the Authorizaton header and should work.

Here's an example of how to accomplish this in javascript:

```javascript

var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', 'https://api.example.com/endpoint', true);
xhr.withCredentials = true;

xhr.onreadystatechange = function() {
  if (xhr.readyState === XMLHttpRequest.DONE) {
    console.log(xhr.responseText);
  }
};

var headers = new Headers();
headers.append('Access-Control-Request-Method', 'GET');
headers.append('Access-Control-Request-Headers', 'Authorization');

xhr.setRequestHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', '*');
xhr.setRequestHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', 'true');
xhr.setRequestHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS');
xhr.setRequestHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Content-Type, Authorization');

xhr.send();
```
